// Battlefield V verisi: scripts/bf-data.ps1 -Game BFV ile Battlefield Wiki'den (CC BY-SA) oluşturulur, elle düzenlenmez.
const BF = {"game":"Battlefield V","weapons":[{"name":"Gewehr 43","group":"Assault","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/e/e6/Battlefield_V_Gewehr_43.png/revision/latest","kit":"Assault","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"300 RPM","magazine":"10 rounds (5-round stripper clips)","damage":"45 - 28","velocity":"760 m/s","ammo":"7.92x57mm Mauser","unlock":"Assault Rank 1","desc":"This rifle is so sturdy it can be used as a ladder rung if needed. The Germans designed it when they saw that the Red Army had adopted semi-automatic rifles."},{"name":"M1A1 Carbine","group":"Assault","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/3/3d/Battlefield_V_M1A1_Carbine.png/revision/latest","kit":"Assault","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"450 RPM","magazine":"15+1 rounds","damage":"27.8 - 18.6","velocity":"610 m/s","ammo":".30 Carbine","unlock":"Assault Rank 5","desc":"The M1A1 was one of the most iconic weapons of World War II. US troops loved it due to its firepower, light weight and small size."},{"name":"Turner SMLE","group":"Assault","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/3/32/Battlefield_V_Turner_SMLE.png/revision/latest","kit":"Assault","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"360 RPM","magazine":"10 rounds","damage":"40 - 23","velocity":"700 m/s","ammo":".303 British","unlock":"Assault Rank 10","desc":"The Turner SMLE had many qualities, but simplicity was not one of them. It did well in extremely cold conditions and the Canadian Army tested it, but they didn\u0027t adopt it."},{"name":"Selbstlader 1916","group":"Assault","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/f/f9/Battlefield_V_Selbstlader_1916.png/revision/latest","kit":"Assault","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"224 RPM","magazine":"25+1 rounds","damage":"45 - 28","velocity":"760 m/s","ammo":"7.92x57mm Mauser","unlock":"Assault Rank 16","desc":"The Selbstlader 1916 was an early German semi-automatic rifle whose sensitivity to field conditions made it more popular with the German air force than its army. Only 1000 were ever made."},{"name":"Gewehr 1-5","group":"Assault","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/d/db/Battlefield_V_Gewehr_1-5.png/revision/latest","kit":"Assault","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"360 RPM","magazine":"30+1 rounds","damage":"36.0 - 18.6","velocity":"580 m/s","ammo":"7.92x33mm Kurz","unlock":"Assault Rank 20","desc":"The German military created the Volksturmgewehr to arm the German civilians with as a last line of defense. It was a semi-automatic rifle and used the same detachable magazine as the StG 44."},{"name":"Ag m/42","group":"Assault","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/6/62/Battlefield_V_Ag_m42.png/revision/latest","kit":"Assault","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"450 RPM","magazine":"10 rounds","damage":"27.8 - 23.0","velocity":"780 m/s","ammo":"6.5x55mm Swedish","unlock":"","desc":"The Swedish army fielded a limited number of m/42 during World War II. Some rifles found their way to Norway immediately after the war, and it\u0027s possible that the m/42 was also used by resistance groups."},{"name":"MAS 44","group":"Assault","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/9/97/Battlefield_V_MAS_44.png/revision/latest","kit":"Assault","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"360 RPM","magazine":"10 rounds","damage":"40 - 23","velocity":"820 m/s","ammo":"7.5x54mm French","unlock":"","desc":"The MAS 44 was a French secret so well-kept from the German Army that it barely survived. Samples ended up in the hands of partisans. It didn\u0027t enter mass production until after the war."},{"name":"Karabin 1938M","group":"Assault","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/f/ff/Battlefield_V_Karabin_1938M.png/revision/latest","kit":"Assault","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"300 RPM","magazine":"10 Rounds (5 Round Clips)","damage":"45 - 28","velocity":"760 m/s","ammo":"7.92x57mm IS","unlock":"","desc":"Influenced by the BAR, this Polish made rifle was in early troop trials as war broke out. A semi-automatic, fixed magazine rifle reminiscent of popular carbines that were already in use."},{"name":"M1 Garand","group":"Assault","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/f/f9/Battlefield_V_M1_Garand.png/revision/latest","kit":"Assault","fire":"Semi-Automatic, Single Shot (Grenade Launcher)","rof":"360 RPM","magazine":"8 rounds","damage":"Default","velocity":"780 m/s","ammo":".30-06","unlock":"","desc":"A very reliable semi-automatic rifle that was standard issue for American infantry during the war. Probably best known for the en-bloc clip it utilizes. or rather the distinct sound it makes when the last round is fired."},{"name":"M1941 Johnson","group":"Assault","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/4/40/Battlefield_V_M1941_Johnson.png/revision/latest","kit":"Assault","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"257 RPM","magazine":"10 rounds","damage":"45 - 28","velocity":"780 m/s","ammo":".30-06","unlock":"","desc":"A semi-automatic rifle that featured a rotating bolt and a cylindrical magazine. The reduced weight at the front of the rifle compared to other rifles at the time made easy handling [sic]. While never formally adopted by US forces, a number of them found their way into troops\u0027 hands."},{"name":"Sturmgewehr 1-5","group":"Assault","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/6/68/Battlefield_V_Sturmgewehr_1-5.png/revision/latest","kit":"Assault","fire":"Fully Automatic, Semi-Automatic","rof":"670 RPM","magazine":"30+1 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 15.0","velocity":"620 m/s","ammo":"7.92x33mm Kurz","unlock":"Default","desc":"The Sturmgewehr 1-5 was the selective-fire variant of the Gewehr 1-5 semi-automatic rifle. As the concept of a fully automatic 1-5 was ultimately rejected, very few were produced."},{"name":"M1907 SF","group":"Assault","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/c/c5/Battlefield_V_M1907_SF.png/revision/latest","kit":"Assault","fire":"Fully Automatic, Semi-Automatic","rof":"770 RPM","magazine":"20+1 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 15.0","velocity":"570 m/s","ammo":".351 Winchester Self-Loading","unlock":"Assault Rank 8","desc":"The M1907 SF was similar to the M1 carbine, only heavier and more powerful. Some versions were modified so that they could fire on full auto. It was mainly used by the French and the British."},{"name":"StG 44","group":"Assault","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/a/a5/Battlefield_V_StG_44.png/revision/latest","kit":"Assault","fire":"Automatic, Semi-Automatic","rof":"600 RPM","magazine":"30 + 1 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 15.0","velocity":"620 m/s","ammo":"7.92x33mm Kurz","unlock":"Assault Rank 13","desc":"Many consider the Sturmgewehr 44 to be the first real assault rifle. It combined a high rate of fire with high accuracy and stopping power. Several modern weapons are based on its design."},{"name":"Ribeyrolles 1918","group":"Assault","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/2/28/Battlefield_V_Ribeyrolles_1918.png/revision/latest","kit":"Assault","fire":"Automatic, Semi-Automatic","rof":"540 RPM","magazine":"25 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 15.0","velocity":"600 m/s","ammo":"8x35mm Ribeyrolles","unlock":"","desc":"With the Ribeyrolles, the French tried to produce an early automatic rifle. Equipped with a detachable magazine and a light bipod, the Ribeyrolles never entered mass-production."},{"name":"Breda M1935 PG","group":"Assault","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/6/67/Battlefield_V_Breda_M1935_PG.png/revision/latest","kit":"Assault","fire":"4-Round Burst, Semi-Automatic","rof":"Total","magazine":"20 Rounds","damage":"30.0 - 22.0","velocity":"700 m/s","ammo":"Mauser 7x57mm","unlock":"","desc":"The first burst assault rifle of the era. Fires an accurate and deadly 4-round burst."},{"name":"M2 Carbine","group":"Assault","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/e/e8/Battlefield_V_M2_Carbine.png/revision/latest","kit":"Assault","fire":"Fully Automatic, Semi-Automatic","rof":"830 RPM","magazine":"30+1 rounds","damage":"20.1 - 13.0","velocity":"610 m/s","ammo":".30 Carbine Auto","unlock":"Default","desc":"After the success of the M1 carbine this select fire version was developed that would allow fully automatic fire. The design was intended to deliver a similar firepower capability to the German fully automatic rifles in use at the time."},{"name":"STEN","group":"Medic","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/0/07/Battlefield_V_STEN.png/revision/latest","kit":"Medic","fire":"Automatic","rof":"540 RPM","magazine":"32 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 12.0","velocity":"495 m/s","ammo":"9x19mm Parabellum","unlock":"Default","desc":"The famous Sten submachine gun was wildly popular with the British army during World War II. It compensated for its low accuracy with a high rate of fire. It was a cheap and simple design that proved easy to mass-produce."},{"name":"Suomi KP/-31","group":"Medic","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/8/8b/Battlefield_V_Suomi_KP-31.png/revision/latest","kit":"Medic","fire":"Automatic, Semi-Automatic","rof":"770 RPM","magazine":"20 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 12.0","velocity":"330 m/s","ammo":"9x19mm Parabellum","unlock":"Medic Rank 1","desc":"The KP/-31 was possibly the best submachine gun of World War II. It had great accuracy and a high rate of fire. The Russians copied the design, but their version never reached the same high standard."},{"name":"MP40","group":"Medic","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/6/60/Battlefield_V_MP40.png/revision/latest","kit":"Medic","fire":"Automatic","rof":"540 RPM","magazine":"32 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 12.0","velocity":"455 m/s","ammo":"9x19mm Parabellum","unlock":"Medic Rank 6","desc":"The Germans used this gun in great numbers. The Allies called it the \"Schmeisser\", but the weapon designer Hugo Schmeisser was never involved in its design."},{"name":"MP28","group":"Medic","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/f/fb/Battlefield_V_MP28.png/revision/latest","kit":"Medic","fire":"Automatic","rof":"670 RPM","magazine":"30 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 12.0","velocity":"345 m/s","ammo":"9x19mm Parabellum","unlock":"Medic Rank 10","desc":"After World War I, Hugo Schmeisser made many improvements to the MP18, such as replacing the drum magazine with a straight one. The resulting MP28 was sold to both China and Japan among others."},{"name":"EMP","group":"Medic","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/9/90/Battlefield_V_EMP.png/revision/latest","kit":"Medic","fire":"Automatic, Semi-Automatic","rof":"568 RPM","magazine":"32 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 12.0","velocity":"420 m/s","ammo":"9x19mm Parabellum","unlock":"Medic Rank 13","desc":"This submachine gun got around. The Germans built it and the Spaniards used it in the Spanish Civil War. Later both the French and the Germans deployed it."},{"name":"MP34","group":"Medic","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/7/75/Battlefield_V_MP34.png/revision/latest","kit":"Medic","fire":"Automatic, Semi-Automatic","rof":"514 RPM","magazine":"20 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 13.0","velocity":"495 m/s","ammo":"9x25mm Mauser","unlock":"Medic Rank 16","desc":"The MP34\u0027s design was based on the old MP18 submachine gun. Used primarily by the German army, it was an extremely well-made gun although the production costs were very high."},{"name":"M1928A1","group":"Medic","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/9/9b/Battlefield_V_M1928A1.png/revision/latest","kit":"Medic","fire":"Automatic","rof":"720 RPM","magazine":"20 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 12.0","velocity":"330 m/s","ammo":".45 ACP","unlock":"Medic Rank 20","desc":"The Chicago gangsters made the \"Tommy Gun\" famous in the 1920s. The US Army adopted it in 1938 for its accuracy and high rate of fire, and Allied Commando and Ranger units used it extensively."},{"name":"ZK-383","group":"Medic","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/9/97/Battlefield_V_ZK-383.png/revision/latest","kit":"Medic","fire":"Automatic, Semi-Automatic","rof":"514 RPM","magazine":"30 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 12.0","velocity":"495 m/s","ammo":"9x19mm Parabellum","unlock":"","desc":"Firing pistol rounds, the German Army used the ZK-383 as a submachine gun despite it having many of the features found in a full-fledged machine gun, such as a detachable barrel and integrated bipod."},{"name":"MAB 38","group":"Medic","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/6/63/Battlefield_V_MAB_38.png/revision/latest","kit":"Medic","fire":"Automatic, Semi-Automatic","rof":"600 RPM","magazine":"20 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 12.0","velocity":"345 m/s","ammo":"9x19mm Parabellum","unlock":"","desc":"The MAB38 is a submachine gun used by the Italian military during World War 2. Other countries used it into the 1960s."},{"name":"Type 100","group":"Medic","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/8/8f/Battlefield_V_Type_100.png/revision/latest","kit":"Medic","fire":"Automatic","rof":"720 RPM","magazine":"30 rounds","damage":"20.1 - 10.1","velocity":"420 m/s","ammo":"8mm Nambu","unlock":"Default","desc":"Perhaps the only submachine gun manufactured within Japan in any significant quantity, it utilized a smaller caliber ammunition than most other SMGs of the time."},{"name":"Type 2A","group":"Medic","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/8/82/Battlefield_V_Type_2A.png/revision/latest","kit":"Medic","fire":"Automatic","rof":"1,028 RPM","magazine":"30+1 Rounds","damage":"20.1 - 10.1","velocity":"315 m/s","ammo":"8x22mm HighROF","unlock":"","desc":"An experimental submachine gun design put into production due to the urgent need of automatic firing infantry weapons."},{"name":"M3 Grease Gun","group":"Medic","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/5/59/Battlefield_V_M3_Grease_Gun.png/revision/latest","kit":"Medic","fire":"Automatic","rof":"450 RPM","magazine":"30 rounds","damage":"33.4 - 13.5","velocity":"345 m/s","ammo":".45ACP M3","unlock":"","desc":"Light, cheap, and easy to manufacture were the goals when designing this submachine gun. The result ended up being an effective weapon for many years after its initial production."},{"name":"Welgun","group":"Medic","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/e/ef/Battlefield_V_Welgun.png/revision/latest","kit":"Medic","fire":"Automatic","rof":"568 RPM","magazine":"32 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 12.0","velocity":"420 m/s","ammo":"9x19mm Parabellum","unlock":"","desc":"The Special Operations Executive had many projects going during the war. Among these was the Welgun, a submachine gun that would be easy to use and cheap to produce. Only a few prototypes were made."},{"name":"Commando Carbine","group":"Medic","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/0/0b/Battlefield_V_Commando_Carbine.png/revision/latest","kit":"Medic","fire":"Bolt-Action","rof":"93 RPM","magazine":"7+1 rounds","damage":"60 - 30","velocity":"350 m/s","ammo":".45 ACP","unlock":"","desc":"Despite its French-sounding name, the De Lisle carbine was a British weapon. It had an integrated suppressor, which was combined with subsonic ammo to make it one of the quietest firearms ever."},{"name":"M28 con Tromboncino","group":"Medic","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/3/31/Battlefield_V_M28_con_Tromboncino.png/revision/latest","kit":"Medic","fire":"Bolt-Action, Single Shot (Grenade Launcher)","rof":"87 RPM","magazine":"6 rounds (6 round en-bloc clip)","damage":"56 - 40","velocity":"500 m/s","ammo":"6.5x52mm Carcano","unlock":"","desc":"A modernized M91 carbine that comes with a grenade launcher, useful both against infantry and vehicles."},{"name":"Jungle Carbine","group":"Medic","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/6/69/Battlefield_V_Jungle_Carbine.png/revision/latest","kit":"Medic","fire":"Bolt-Action","rof":"84 RPM","magazine":"10 rounds (5-round clip)","damage":"56 - 40","velocity":"500 m/s","ammo":".303 British","unlock":"","desc":"Formally called \"Rifle No.5 Mk I\", the No.5 Jungle Carbine is based on the Lee-Enfield No.4 Mk I."},{"name":"KE7","group":"Support","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/b/ba/Battlefield_V_KE7.png/revision/latest","kit":"Support","fire":"Automatic, Semi-Automatic","rof":"568 RPM","magazine":"25 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 15.0","velocity":"740 m/s","ammo":"7.92x57mm Mauser","unlock":"Default","desc":"Even though this light machine gun was designed in Switzerland, the Swiss army never used it. It had a quick-change barrel and a selective fire mode similar to that on many submachine guns."},{"name":"Bren LMG","group":"Support","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/f/f2/Bren_LMG_Icon_BF1942.png/revision/latest","kit":"Assault","fire":"Automatic","rof":"540 RPM","magazine":"30 rounds","damage":"9","velocity":"1,000 m/s","ammo":"barProjectile","unlock":"","desc":"The British built this light machine gun based on a Czechoslovak design. It had a top-mounted magazine which gave it a distinct look. It was very popular with the troops."},{"name":"Lewis Gun","group":"Support","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/9/9c/Battlefield_V_Lewis_Gun.png/revision/latest","kit":"Support","fire":"Automatic","rof":"540 RPM","magazine":"47 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 15.0","velocity":"740 m/s","ammo":".303 British","unlock":"Support Rank 10","desc":"The Lewis Gun entered Allied service in 1914. In World War II, the British again used it as an anti-aircraft gun. Its barrel shroud and top-mounted pan magazine made it easy to recognize."},{"name":"Fallschirmjager Gewehr 42","group":"Support","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/c/cb/Fallschirmj%C3%A4gergewehr_42_Icon_BF1942.png/revision/latest","kit":"Assault","fire":"Automatic","rof":"540 RPM","magazine":"30 rounds","damage":"7","velocity":"1,000 m/s","ammo":"sg44Projectile","unlock":"","desc":"With its selective fire functionality the FG 42 was way ahead of its time. German paratroopers used and liked it, but it was never produced in great numbers."},{"name":"LS/26","group":"Support","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/f/f9/Battlefield_V_LS26.png/revision/latest","kit":"Support","fire":"Automatic","rof":"600 RPM","magazine":"20 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 15.0","velocity":"800 m/s","ammo":"7.62x53mmR","unlock":"","desc":"A Finnish light machine gun that saw use in the Winter and Continuation War against the Soviet Union."},{"name":"Madsen MG","group":"Support","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/9/97/Battlefield_V_Madsen_MG.png/revision/latest","kit":"Support","fire":"Automatic, Semi-Automatic","rof":"514 RPM","magazine":"25 Rounds","damage":"22.0 - 18.0","velocity":"780 m/s","ammo":"Mauser 6.5x55mm","unlock":"","desc":"The Danish-made Madsen was introduced in 1902. It was the first light machine gun in wide use. Like the Bren it had a top-mounted magazine, and were the standard LMG issued by the Norwegian army. [sic]"},{"name":"BAR M1918A2","group":"Support","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/2/2d/Battlefield_V_BAR_M1918A2.png/revision/latest","kit":"Support","fire":"Automatic - High RoF, Automatic - Low RoF","rof":"720 RPM (High RoF)","magazine":"20 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 15.0","velocity":"740 m/s","ammo":".30-06","unlock":"","desc":"An updated version of an automatic rifle that was developed during the Great War. The new version made reloading easier and could be used with a bipod."},{"name":"Type 97 MG","group":"Support","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/6/68/Battlefield_V_Type_97_MG.png/revision/latest","kit":"Support","fire":"Automatic","rof":"568 RPM","magazine":"25 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 15.0","velocity":"740 m/s","ammo":"7.7x58mm Arisaka","unlock":"","desc":"A machine gun based upon a Czech design, modified for use in Japanese tanks. It was less commonly used as an infantry weapon due to the excessive weight."},{"name":"Type 11 LMG","group":"Support","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/5/54/Battlefield_V_Type_11_LMG.png/revision/latest","kit":"Support","fire":"Automatic","rof":"514 RPM","magazine":"30 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 15.0","velocity":"740 m/s","ammo":"6.5×50mm Arisaka","unlock":"","desc":"This early Japanese machine gun design included a unique offset hopper feed system that utilized the same cartridge clips as some of the Japanese infantry rifles simplifying ammunition logistics on the battlefield."},{"name":"Chauchat","group":"Support","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/6/62/Battlefield_V_Chauchat.png/revision/latest","kit":"Support","fire":"Automatic, Semi-Automatic","rof":"327 RPM","magazine":"20 Rounds","damage":"35.0 - 22.0","velocity":"720 m/s","ammo":"8mm Lebel","unlock":"","desc":"In World War I, the standard French light machine gun was called the Chauchat. It could be handled by a crew of just two, which was rare at the time. It had many features seen on later LMGs."},{"name":"MG 34","group":"Support","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/8/83/Battlefield_V_MG_34.png/revision/latest","kit":"Support","fire":"Automatic, Semi-Automatic","rof":"670 RPM","magazine":"50 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 15.0","velocity":"740 m/s","ammo":"7.92x57mm Mauser","unlock":"Support Rank 1","desc":"The MG34 was the first of a kind: a portable, air-cooled machine gun with a high rate of fire. Too complex to properly mass-produce, it was later supplanted by the MG42."},{"name":"MG 42","group":"Support","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/8/81/Battlefield_V_MG_42.png/revision/latest","kit":"Support","fire":"Automatic","rof":"981 RPM","magazine":"50 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 15.0","velocity":"740 m/s","ammo":"7.92x57mm","unlock":"Support Rank 20","desc":"This ground-breaking machine gun was reliable, easy to use, and had an extreme rate of fire. Due to the sound made by the high firing rate, the Allies nicknamed the MG42 \"Hitler\u0027s Buzzsaw\". The MG42 saw action on all fronts."},{"name":"VGO","group":"Support","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/8/8d/Battlefield_V_VGO.png/revision/latest","kit":"Support","fire":"Automatic","rof":"830 RPM","magazine":"60 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 15.0","velocity":"740 m/s","ammo":".303 British","unlock":"","desc":"The VGO began life as an airplane-mounted machine gun. But it was soon adopted for land use. A high rate of fire made it a favorite among Allied commandos units."},{"name":"M1922 MG","group":"Support","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/7/70/Battlefield_V_M1922_MG.png/revision/latest","kit":"Support","fire":"Automatic","rof":"770 RPM","magazine":"150 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 15.0","velocity":"700 m/s","ammo":"7.5x57mm French","unlock":"","desc":"The French never got to field their M1922 light machine gun during World War I. Many variants lived on into World War II however, where they were mainly used in the French Navy."},{"name":"S2-200","group":"Support","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/1/1d/Battlefield_V_S2-200.png/revision/latest","kit":"Support","fire":"Automatic, Semi-Automatic","rof":"770 RPM","magazine":"30 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 15.0","velocity":"740 m/s","ammo":"7.92x57mm","unlock":"","desc":"The S2-200 was magazine-fed machine gun originally banned by the Treaty of Versailles after World War I. Rejected by the German infantry, the Luftwaffe eventually adopted them for use in their aircraft."},{"name":"M1919A6","group":"Support","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/b/b5/Battlefield_V_M1919A6.png/revision/latest","kit":"Support","fire":"Automatic","rof":"600 RPM","magazine":"250 rounds","damage":"25.1 - 15.0","velocity":"800 m/s","ammo":".30-06","unlock":"Default","desc":"The M1919 is mainly known as the .30 cal. machine gun. A bipod and a carrying handle was added which resulted in the A6 model."},{"name":"12g Automatic","group":"Support","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/2/2e/Battlefield_V_12g_Automatic.png/revision/latest","kit":"Support","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"138 RPM","magazine":"4+1 shells","damage":"Buckshot","velocity":"333 m/s","ammo":"12 Gauge","unlock":"Support Rank 5","desc":"The 12g Automatic served in both world wars. It was the first successful semi-automatic shotgun used by the Allies, eventually inspiring the German army to create their own version."},{"name":"M30 Drilling","group":"Support","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/2/20/Battlefield_V_M30_Drilling.png/revision/latest","kit":"Support","fire":"Double-barrel Shotgun, Single-shot Rifle","rof":"Shotgun","magazine":"2 shells (Shotgun)","damage":"Shotgun","velocity":"Shotgun","ammo":"12 Gauge Buckshot","unlock":"Support Rank 13","desc":"In case Luftwaffe pilots got shot down, they had this weapon to both hunt game and protect themselves with. The M30 had two shotgun barrels and one normal rifle barrel."},{"name":"M1897","group":"Support","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/c/c9/Battlefield_V_M1897.png/revision/latest","kit":"Support","fire":"Pump-Action","rof":"100 RPM","magazine":"5 shells","damage":"Buckshot","velocity":"333 m/s","ammo":"12 Gauge Buckshot","unlock":"","desc":"The US-made M1897 is most famously known as the \"Trench Gun\". More than one million were built. It was used by soldiers, policemen and hunters around the world."},{"name":"Model 37","group":"Support","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/d/d8/Battlefield_V_Model_37.png/revision/latest","kit":"Support","fire":"Automatic (Slamfire)","rof":"150 RPM","magazine":"5 shells","damage":"Buckshot","velocity":"333 m/s","ammo":"12 Gauge Buckshot","unlock":"Default","desc":"Pump action shotguns remained popular since their debut in the late 19th century. While the design of this shotgun was kept simple its reliability and effectiveness in combat was renowned."},{"name":"Sjögren Shotgun","group":"Support","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/1/11/Battlefield_V_Sj%C3%B6gren_Shotgun.png/revision/latest","kit":"Support","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"120 RPM","magazine":"4 + 1 shells","damage":"Buckshot","velocity":"333 m/s","ammo":"12 Gauge Buckshot","unlock":"","desc":"While its inertia-based loading system proved to be ahead of its time, the Sjögren\u0027s service in World War II was mostly centered around different resistance groups."},{"name":"Lee-Enfield No.4 Mk I","group":"Recon","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/d/da/Battlefield_V_Lee-Enfield_No._4_Mk_I.png/revision/latest","kit":"Recon","fire":"Bolt-Action","rof":"72 RPM","magazine":"10 rounds (5 rounds clip)","damage":"65 - 50","velocity":"600 m/s","ammo":".303","unlock":"Default","desc":"The Lee Enfield rifle has a long history which goes back to before World War I. The no. 4 model had a brutal bayonet called the \"pigsticker\" by the British troops."},{"name":"Gewehr M95/30","group":"Recon","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/1/17/Battlefield_V_Gewehr_M9530.png/revision/latest","kit":"Recon","fire":"Bolt-Action","rof":"58 RPM","magazine":"5 rounds (En-bloc clip)","damage":"80 - 66","velocity":"750 m/s","ammo":"8x56mmR","unlock":"Recon Rank 6","desc":"The Gewehr M95/30\u0027s origins dated back to the late 19th century and saw heavy use in World War I by the Austro-Hungarian Army. By the WWII, the gun had mainly relegated to secondary militia units."},{"name":"Krag-Jørgensen","group":"Recon","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/5/55/BFV_Krag-Jorgensen_Weapon.png/revision/latest","kit":"Recon","fire":"Bolt-Action","rof":"55 RPM","magazine":"6 rounds","damage":"60","velocity":"900 m/s","ammo":"6.5x55mm","unlock":"Recon Rank 13","desc":"The origins of the Danish Krag-Jørgensen rifle date back to the late 19th century. The gun\u0027s main feature was a special type of integral magazine which loaded from the side rather than the top."},{"name":"Kar98k","group":"Recon","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/c/c7/BFV_Kar98k_Weapon.png/revision/latest","kit":"Recon","fire":"Bolt-Action","rof":"49 RPM","magazine":"5 rounds (5 round clip)","damage":"75 - 66","velocity":"900 m/s","ammo":"7.92x57mm","unlock":"Recon Rank 20","desc":"The Kar-98k was the most common bolt-action rifle in the German army. Millions were produced before and during World War II. Many of them still in use today."},{"name":"Ross Rifle Mk III","group":"Recon","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/5/59/BFV_Ross_Rifle_Weapon.png/revision/latest","kit":"Recon","fire":"Bolt-Action","rof":"64 RPM","magazine":"5 rounds (5-round stripper clip)","damage":"65 - 50","velocity":"750 m/s","ammo":".303 British","unlock":"","desc":"Although the Ross was exceptionally accurate, its length and sensitivity to poor ammunition made the rifle unsuitable for infantry use. It proved very popular with British and Canadian snipers though."},{"name":"Type 99 Arisaka","group":"Recon","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/9/95/BFV_Type_99_Arisaka.PNG/revision/latest","kit":"Recon","fire":"Bolt-Action","rof":"58 RPM","magazine":"5 rounds (5 round clip)","damage":"75 - 66","velocity":"","ammo":"7.7x58mm Arisaka","unlock":"Default","desc":"Equipped with a shorter barrel than most sniper rifles manufactured during the war, a choice that saved cost and materials and was found to only have a nominal impact on its accuracy."},{"name":"K31/43","group":"Recon","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/d/db/BFV_K31-43.PNG/revision/latest","kit":"Recon","fire":"Bolt-Action","rof":"55","magazine":"6 rounds","damage":"60","velocity":"750 m/s","ammo":"7.5x55mm Swiss","unlock":"","desc":"The sniper variant of the Swiss K31 service rifle, the K31/43 has an integral periscopic scope."},{"name":"Model 8","group":"Recon","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/1/15/Battlefield_V_Model_8.png/revision/latest","kit":"Recon","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"163 RPM","magazine":"5 rounds (5 round clip)","damage":"52.1 - 38","velocity":"660 m/s","ammo":".35 Remington","unlock":"Recon Rank 1","desc":"The first successful American-made semi-auto rifle, the Model 8 shares the same long recoil mechanism as the Automatic 5 shotgun."},{"name":"ZH-29","group":"Recon","image":null,"kit":"Recon","fire":"Semi Automatic","rof":"150 rpm","magazine":"5 rounds (Default)","damage":"54 - 52.1","velocity":"760 m/s","ammo":"7.92x57mm Mauser","unlock":"Recon Rank 10","desc":"This was one of the first successful semi-automatic rifles, and its design would go on to inspire the StG-44. The Chinese used it during World War II, but it was developed in Czechoslovakia."},{"name":"RSC","group":"Recon","image":null,"kit":"Recon","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"180 RPM","magazine":"5+1 rounds","damage":"52.1 - 38","velocity":"630 m/s","ammo":"8mm Lebel","unlock":"Recon Rank 16","desc":"By repurposing parts from the Lebel rifle it was designed to replace, the RSC was cheaper to produce for the French army, though there were complaints that it was unwieldy in the trenches."},{"name":"Selbstlader 1906","group":"Recon","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/9/93/BFV_Selbstlader_1906_Weapon.png/revision/latest","kit":"Recon","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"138 RPM","magazine":"5 rounds","damage":"54 - 52.1","velocity":"760 m/s","ammo":"7.92x57mm Mauser","unlock":"","desc":"The same toggle-lock mechanism used on the German\nP 08 pistol was also used for a semi-automatic rifle. The design was a success but only a few prototypes were ever built."},{"name":"M3 Infrared","group":"Recon","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/1/16/Battlefield_V_M3_Infrared.png/revision/latest","kit":"Recon","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"450 RPM","magazine":"15+1 rounds","damage":"27.8 - 18.6","velocity":"610 m/s","ammo":".30 Carbine Suppressed","unlock":"","desc":"Equipped with an infrared spotlight and scope this converted M1A1 Carbine was intended for night fighting. Although the enhanced optics were cutting edge technology, the added weight made the rifle cumbersome."},{"name":"P08 Pistol","group":"Recon","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/4/4f/Lugerp08menu.jpg/revision/latest","kit":"","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"360 RPM","magazine":"8+1 rounds","damage":"25.0 - 14.3","velocity":"350 m/s","ammo":"9x19mm Parabellum","unlock":"Any Class Rank 7","desc":"The P08 pistol is commonly known as the \"Luger\", named after the man who patented it. The German military tried replacing it with the P38 during World War II, but it would remain in production until 1943."},{"name":"Trench Carbine","group":"Recon","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/2/20/BF5_Trench_Carbine.PNG/revision/latest","kit":"Recon","fire":"Semi-Automatic, Fully Automatic (Upgrade)","rof":"450 RPM","magazine":"40+1 Rounds","damage":"36.0 - 13.0","velocity":"","ammo":"9x19mm","unlock":"","desc":"Built as a prototype to fullfil a request from the German army. Although it\u0027s design was based on other popular pistols used during the Great War, the Trench Carbine is unique in many ways. [sic]"},{"name":"Boys AT Rifle","group":"Recon","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/c/cd/BFV_Boys_AT_Rifle.PNG/revision/latest","kit":"Recon","fire":"Bolt-Action","rof":"22 RPM","magazine":"5+1 rounds","damage":"100 - 72","velocity":"400 m/s","ammo":".55 Boys","unlock":"","desc":"A British anti-tank rifle. Proving ineffective against improved axis tanks, it was replaced by the PIAT during the war."},{"name":"Panzerbüchse 39","group":"Recon","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/8/8f/BF5_Panzerbuchse_39.jpeg/revision/latest","kit":"Recon","fire":"Bolt-action","rof":"18 RPM","magazine":"11 Rounds","damage":"112 - 80","velocity":"400 m/s","ammo":"7.92x94mm PzB39","unlock":"","desc":"Compared to most anti-tank rifles, the Panzerbüchse 39 fires a very light projectile at very high velocity, relying on speed rather than mass to penetrate armor."},{"name":"P38 Pistol","group":"Sidearms","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/5/5b/Battlefield_V_P38_Pistol.png/revision/latest","kit":"All","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"359 RPM","magazine":"8 + 1 rounds","damage":"25 - 14.3","velocity":"250 m/s","ammo":"9x19mm Parabellum","unlock":"Default","desc":"The P38 pistol was the standard German sidearm in World War II. Some of its technical solutions are still in use today. It was chambered for the 9mm Parabellum round."},{"name":"Ruby","group":"Sidearms","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/c/c4/Starrubymenu.jpg/revision/latest","kit":"","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"449 RPM","magazine":"9+1 rounds","damage":"25.5 - 12.5","velocity":"250 m/s","ammo":".32 ACP","unlock":"Any Class Rank 3","desc":"The Ruby pistol was incredibly easy to use, which was why it survived long after World War II. It first saw action in World War I, and it was a great back-up weapon in trench warfare."},{"name":"M1911","group":"Sidearms","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/a/a6/Battlefield_V_M1911.png/revision/latest","kit":"All","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"300 RPM","magazine":"7 + 1 rounds","damage":"34 - 13","velocity":"250 m/s","ammo":".45 ACP","unlock":"Any Class Rank 5","desc":"When people think of a .45, they often imagine the M1911 automatic pistol. They were so popular in World War II that several gun manufacturers were needed to meet the demand."},{"name":"Repetierpistole M1912","group":"Sidearms","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/5/57/M1912menu.jpg/revision/latest","kit":"","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"450 RPM","magazine":"8 rounds","damage":"25 - 15","velocity":"350 m/s","ammo":"9x23mm Steyr","unlock":"Any Class Rank 11","desc":"The M1912 was a rugged and dependable pistol with an integral magazine. First used in World War I, the German Army ordered 60,000 pistols when Germany annexed Austria in 1938."},{"name":"Mk VI Revolver","group":"Sidearms","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/b/bd/Battlefield_V_Mk_VI_Revolver.png/revision/latest","kit":"All","fire":"Double-Action","rof":"180 RPM","magazine":"6 rounds","damage":"51 - 16.6","velocity":"230 m/s","ammo":".455 Webley Mk II","unlock":"Any Class Rank 15","desc":"The Mk VI was the standard revolver for the British army in both World War I and World War II. The earliest version, the Mk I, was the first put into service by the army in 1887."},{"name":"Liberator","group":"Sidearms","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/a/a8/Liberatormenu.jpg/revision/latest","kit":"","fire":"Single-Shot","rof":"9 RPM","magazine":"1 round","damage":"34 - 13","velocity":"250m/s","ammo":".45 ACP","unlock":"Any Class Rank 19","desc":"The Americans created the Liberator pistol for use by resistance groups. Simple to manufacture, it was known for its single-shot functionality. The Allies gave large numbers to the French resistance."},{"name":"Type 94","group":"Sidearms","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/e/e9/BFV_Type_94.PNG/revision/latest","kit":"","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"450 RPM","magazine":"6+1 rounds","damage":"25.5 - 15","velocity":"250 m/s","ammo":"8mm Nambu","unlock":"War In The Pacific Rank 23","desc":"Manufactured in Japan since before the war, some viewed this pistol as being unnecessarily complex in its design while others admired that it was compact and lightweight."},{"name":"Model 27","group":"Sidearms","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/6/64/BFV_Model_27.png/revision/latest","kit":"","fire":"Double-Action","rof":"105 RPM","magazine":"6 rounds","damage":"56.0 - 28.0","velocity":"460 m/s","ammo":".357 Magnum","unlock":"War in the Pacific Chapter Rank 30","desc":"Utilized by several law enforcement agencies in the United States before the war it remained popular due to its renowned reliability and effectiveness at longer ranges than other pistols."},{"name":"PPK","group":"Sidearms","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/b/b3/BFV_PPK.PNG/revision/latest","kit":"","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"449 RPM","magazine":"7+1 rounds","damage":"25.5 - 12.5","velocity":"250 m/s","ammo":".32 ACP","unlock":"","desc":"Designed in 1929, the PPK is the short version of the PP. While the newer P38 was the standard sidearm of German military during World War II the PPK was adopted in small numbers throughout the war."},{"name":"Welrod","group":"Sidearms","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/3/3d/BFV_Welrod.png/revision/latest","kit":"","fire":"Bolt-Action","rof":"42 RPM","magazine":"5+1 rounds","damage":"56 - 36","velocity":"250 m/s","ammo":"9x19mm","unlock":"","desc":"The British developed the Welrod pistol to be as quiet as possible. It was bolt operated and magazine fed. At only 73 dBA it was about as loud as a present day car."},{"name":"Handgun","group":"Others","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/a/a8/BFV_Finger_Gun.jpeg/revision/latest","kit":"","fire":"Semi Automatic","rof":"","magazine":"10 rounds","damage":"","velocity":"","ammo":"","unlock":"","desc":"The Handgun is an easter egg weapon in Battlefield V that was introduced in a patch on June 25, 2019."},{"name":"M1903","group":"Cut Weapons","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/7/74/Springfield_BF1943.png/revision/latest","kit":"Scout","fire":"","rof":"30 rpm","magazine":"5 rounds","damage":"2 shots to kill","velocity":"","ammo":"","unlock":"","desc":"An American bolt-action rifle firing a low drag projectile, excellent for very long ranges."},{"name":"Mle 1903","group":"Cut Weapons","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/3/3f/Mle_1903_Icon_Color.png/revision/latest","kit":"","fire":"Semi-Automatic","rof":"360 RPM","magazine":"7 + 1 rounds","damage":"30 - 13.5","velocity":"350 m/s","ammo":"9x20mmSR Browning Long","unlock":"","desc":"A Belgian-made semi-automatic pistol adopted by a number of nations, including Sweden."},{"name":"Ludwig\u0027s Uber Loaded","group":"Cut Weapons","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/0/0d/Ludwigs_Uber_Loaded.png/revision/latest","kit":"","fire":"","rof":"450 rounds per minute","magazine":"12 rounds","damage":"Close range 5-9","velocity":"300.0","ammo":"","unlock":"","desc":"More rounds and critical hit chance than the original Ludwig\u0027s Loaded!"},{"name":"Trench Carbine","group":"Cut Weapons","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/2/20/BF5_Trench_Carbine.PNG/revision/latest","kit":"Recon","fire":"Semi-Automatic, Fully Automatic (Upgrade)","rof":"450 RPM","magazine":"40+1 Rounds","damage":"36.0 - 13.0","velocity":"","ammo":"9x19mm","unlock":"","desc":"Built as a prototype to fullfil a request from the German army. Although it\u0027s design was based on other popular pistols used during the Great War, the Trench Carbine is unique in many ways. [sic]"}],"maps":[{"name":"Aerodrome","group":"Battlefield V","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/9/96/Battlefield_V_Aerodrome_Article_Header.png/revision/latest","modes":["Breakthrough","Conquest","Team Deathmatch","Grand Operations","Breakthrough","Final Stand","Domination","Frontlines","Outpost"],"place":"Outskirts of ,","date":"November 1942","terrain":"Desert","desc":"The North African desert heat reaches unprecedented levels in the firefights of one of Battlefield V’s launch maps, an Axis airfield set ablaze by a recent Allied bombardment. Welcome to Aerodrome."},{"name":"Arras","group":"Battlefield V","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/8/8a/Battlefield_V_Arras_Article_Header.png/revision/latest","modes":["Breakthrough","Conquest","Squad Conquest","Team Deathmatch","Grand Operations","Frontlines","Final Stand","Grand Operations","Rush","Final Stand","Domination","Frontlines","Outpost","Rush","Combined Arms"],"place":", France","date":"May 21st, 1940","terrain":"Rural","desc":"It’s seemingly serene, this lush French village and its pictorial houses, kissed by the sun and hugged by wall-climbing vegetation. But the scars of Arras run deep – and yet another World War is about to rattle the serenity."},{"name":"Devastation","group":"Battlefield V","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/e/ed/Battlefield_V_Devastation_Article_Header.png/revision/latest","modes":["Breakthrough","Conquest","Squad Conquest","Team Deathmatch","Grand Operations","Frontlines","Final Stand","Domination","Frontlines","Rush","Grind","Fortress"],"place":"Rotterdam, Netherlands","date":"May 14th, 1940","terrain":"Urban","desc":"Rotterdam’s beautiful and complex architecture built tall by hands and machinery now rests scattered as heaps of rubble in Battlefield V’s Devastation map. The range, trajectory, and explosive yield of bombs dropped from above have reshaped the city into a no-man’s-land of ash and remains for soldiers to fight over."},{"name":"Fjell 652","group":"Battlefield V","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/8/82/Battlefield_V_Fjell_652_Article_Header.png/revision/latest","modes":["Breakthrough","Conquest","Squad Conquest","Team Deathmatch","Grand Operations","Conquest","Final Stand","Domination","Combined Arms"],"place":"Bjørnefjell, East of Narvik, Norway","date":"June 1st, 1940","terrain":"Mountainous","desc":"Once a haven for skiers and climbers, Fjell 652 has become a frozen battleground. Narrow pathways wind around craggy barriers, forcing attackers to tread carefully, and giving defenders the perfect cover for an ambush. Fortifications can mean the difference between holding and losing precious ground, and anti-air emplacements are the best defense against death from above. Battle here among the clouds is as bitter, and ferocious as the blizzards that strike without warning."},{"name":"Hamada","group":"Battlefield V","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/e/ee/Battlefield_V_Hamada_Article_Header.jpg/revision/latest","modes":["Breakthrough","Conquest","Squad Conquest","Team Deathmatch","Grand Operations","Airborne","Conquest Assault","Domination","Frontlines","Fortress","Combined Arms"],"place":",","date":"June 15th - 17th, 1941","terrain":"Rocky Desert, Mountainous","desc":"“Hamada” is Arabic for a barren landscape of gravel and bare rock, and it perfectly describes the map. A towering aqueduct-like bridge spans the dry ravine, one of the few signs that remain of a once-thriving civilization. There was life here, once, too – now all lies in ruins and rubble, ground to dust beneath tank treads and infantry boots."},{"name":"Narvik","group":"Battlefield V","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/2/2f/Battlefield_V_Narvik_Article_Header.png/revision/latest","modes":["Breakthrough","Conquest","Squad Conquest","Team Deathmatch","Grand Operations","Airborne","Breakthrough","Domination","Frontlines","Rush","Grind"],"place":"Port of Narvik, Norway","date":"May 28th, 1940","terrain":"Coastal, Urban","desc":"This once charming harbor town now echoes with the sounds of gunfire and munitions. Planes scream overhead, peppering the ground with fire and a high-pitched whistle is the only warning you get before an oncoming bomb tears its way through buildings, vehicles, and anyone unlucky enough to be in its path. Some houses still stand, while others lay in smoldering ruins. Tanks prowl the town’s narrow streets, winding their way up from the harbor to the snowy ridge above, leaving nothing but destruction in their wake."},{"name":"Rotterdam","group":"Battlefield V","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/b/b9/Battlefield_V_Rotterdam_Article_Header.png/revision/latest","modes":["Breakthrough","Conquest","Squad Conquest","Team Deathmatch","Grand Operations","Airborne","Breakthrough","Domination","Frontlines","Rush","Grind","Combined Arms"],"place":"South-west part of Rotterdam, Netherlands","date":"May 10th - 14th, 1940","terrain":"Urban","desc":"All trains through Rotterdam are currently delayed due to an unforeseen military invasion. This Battlefield V map is the stage for an impromptu metropolitan frontline in the war against invading enemy forces. The train cars have been commandeered as makeshift defenses, and the windows of a multi-story building are now opportunities to ambush foes patrolling the maze of streets below."},{"name":"Twisted Steel","group":"Battlefield V","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/3/35/BF5_Twisted_Steel_Article_Header.png/revision/latest","modes":["Breakthrough","Conquest","Domination","Frontlines","Team Deathmatch","Grand Operations","Airborne","Breakthrough","Outpost","Rush","Grind","Combined Arms"],"place":", France","date":"August - September 1944","terrain":"Rural, Marshland","desc":"A flooded French marshland that is overlooked by the largest structure ever created for a Battlefield game – a monumental, partly destroyed steel bridge. This is Twisted Steel."},{"name":"Panzerstorm","group":"Overture","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/1/19/Battlefield_V_Panzerstorm.png/revision/latest","modes":["Breakthrough","Conquest","Domination","Team Deathmatch","Grand Operations","Airborne","Breakthrough"],"place":", Belgium","date":"September 1944","terrain":"Rural","desc":"Set to land with Tides of War Chapter 1: Overture is the Panzerstorm map, a massive terrain of rolling fields excellent for vehicular combat. Mobilize your tanks and carve a path of destruction for a different type of countryside tour."},{"name":"Halvøy","group":"Trial By Fire","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/a/ac/BFV_Halvoy_Map.jpeg/revision/latest","modes":["Firestorm"],"place":"Norway","date":"","terrain":"Mountainous","desc":"At the gathering point for a now evacuated village sits an old church whose bell tower can still be heard from afar. Tall windows can provide sights to the surroundings with an excellent sniper’s nest in the tower. There are multiple routes in and out of the church for soldiers, and those brazen enough to loot the exposed gravestones can find valuable gear."},{"name":"Mercury","group":"Trial By Fire","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/7/77/Bfv_mercury_new.jpg/revision/latest","modes":["Breakthrough","Conquest","Squad Conquest","Team Deathmatch","Domination","Frontlines","Outpost","Rush"],"place":", Greece","date":"May 20th - June 1st, 1941","terrain":"Hilly, Coastal","desc":"On May 30, the fight moves to the Mediterranean as a brand-new map becomes part of the ever-evolving Battlefield V. Prepare for a violent struggle along a beautiful coastline. This is Mercury."},{"name":"Al Sundan","group":"Defying The Odds","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/6/6f/BF5_Al_Sundan.png/revision/latest","modes":["Breakthrough","Conquest","Frontlines","Team Deathmatch","Squad Conquest"],"place":"Mediterranean coast, North Africa","date":"1942","terrain":"Desert Coastline","desc":"Maps, more maps, and further maps were revealed at EA PLAY a few weeks back when Battlefield V took the stage. Arriving June 27, Al Sundan is first up of these new fighting grounds."},{"name":"Marita","group":"Defying The Odds","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/8/84/BF5_Marita_New.png/revision/latest","modes":["Breakthrough","Conquest","Squad Conquest","Team Deathmatch","Domination","Frontlines"],"place":", Greek- border, Greece","date":"Spring 1941","terrain":"Urban, Mountainous","desc":"A once idyllic mountain village in Greece becomes the latest brutal front as the Marita map arrives in Battlefield V."},{"name":"Lofoten Islands","group":"Defying The Odds","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/7/76/BF5_Lofoten_Islands_Promotional.png/revision/latest","modes":["Squad Conquest","Team Deathmatch","Domination"],"place":", Norway","date":"March 4th, 1941","terrain":"Coastal","desc":"The map takes place on the Lofoten Islands in the late spring of 1941 and is loosely based on Operation Claymore. This British commando operation was tasked with destroying vital, Axis-held fish oil factories on the islands. The oil was being shipped to Germany, where glycerine was extracted and used to manufacture high explosives. On this map, combat breaks out at both a fish oil factory (on the first island) and in a nearby village (on the second island)."},{"name":"Provence","group":"Defying The Odds","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/d/d7/Provence_Loading.png/revision/latest","modes":["Breakthrough","Conquest","Domination","Team Deathmatch","Squad Conquest"],"place":", France","date":"August 15th - September 14th, 1944","terrain":"Rural, Urban","desc":"For the Summer Update, we’ve done a major redesign of this battlefield. Provence’s play area has been greatly expanded to enable land vehicles and the possibility to play Conquest and Breakthrough. On top of the new playable area across the river from town, you’ll notice other changes in setting, such as going to battle in early morning – and at a later stage of the war."},{"name":"Operation Underground","group":"Battlefest","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/5/5a/BF5_Operation_Underground_Promotional.jpg/revision/latest","modes":["Breakthrough","Conquest","Squad Conquest","Team Deathmatch","Rush","Grind"],"place":"Germany","date":"Spring 1945","terrain":"Urban, Underground","desc":"A spiritual successor to Battlefield 3’s Operation Métro, Operation Underground is a true passion project when it comes to level design. With our original designer of Métro at the helm, Operation Underground keeps the former map’s intense close-quarter combat in a highly recognizable environment and removes bottlenecks that could make a match stale. In addition, new flanking routes and gameplay dynamics are added to double down on the fun."},{"name":"Iwo Jima","group":"War in the Pacific","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/2/25/BF5_Iwo_Jima_Promotional.jpg/revision/latest","modes":["Breakthrough","Conquest","Squad Conquest","Team Deathmatch","Outpost"],"place":", , Japanese Empire","date":"February 19th - March 26th, 1945","terrain":"Coastal, Moutainous","desc":"The U.S. is attacking Iwo Jima on all fronts, and in this beachlanding mayhem you’ll be engaged in a fight over the thinner part of the island. It’s an all or nothing clash across wide-open black beaches and close-range environments like the tunnels of the mighty Mount Suribachi, inviting you to an iconic king-of-the-hill battle. Landing boats, aircraft, and tanks are plentiful, and each can sway the match in their own way. Push the enemy towards the final objective or hold back the onslaught on this highly dynamic map."},{"name":"Pacific Storm","group":"War in the Pacific","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/4/4a/BF5_Pacific_Storm_Promotional.jpg/revision/latest","modes":["Breakthrough","Conquest","Squad Conquest","Team Deathmatch","Outpost"],"place":"","date":"January 31st - February 23rd, 1944","terrain":"Coastal Islands, Jungle","desc":"The weather is as treacherous as your foes on this set of islands where a game-changing storm may roll in, reducing your sightlines and evolving the battle. Move between islands with your squad and secure the control points using boats, amphibious tanks, or aircraft. As you attack or defend across land, air, and sea – stay on your toes! You never know what’s around the next ridge. Fans of Battlefield 4 will see shades of the classic map Paracel Storm in the world of possibilities offered through Pacific Storm’s layout."},{"name":"Wake Island","group":"War in the Pacific","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/9/95/Battlefield_V_Wake_Island_Article_Header.jpg/revision/latest","modes":["Breakthrough","Conquest Assault","Squad Conquest","Team Deathmatch","Rush"],"place":"","date":"","terrain":"Coastal, Island","desc":"Welcome back to the battlefield that started it all. Welcome back to Wake Island.\nAs Chapter 5: War in the Pacific continues, we’re now deploying our reimagination of what is arguably the most iconic Battlefield map ever. As you fight for control over land, air, and sea on this horseshoe-shaped classic, we hope you’ll enjoy both the nostalgia and the new improvements."},{"name":"Solomon Islands","group":"Into The Jungle","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/8/82/BF5_Solomon_Islands.jpg/revision/latest","modes":["Breakthrough","Conquest","Squad Conquest","Team Deathmatch"],"place":"/,","date":"January 1942 - August 21st, 1945","terrain":"Riverine, Jungle","desc":"Danger and opportunity are everywhere. On Solomon Islands, available February 4, you’ll fight through a jungle environment where you never know what’s lurking around the riverbend, by the dark mangroves, or in the hidden bunkers."},{"name":"Al Marj Encampment","group":"Summer Update","image":"https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/battlefield/images/d/dc/Battlefield_V_Libya_Map.jpeg/revision/latest","modes":["Breakthrough","Conquest","Domination","Team Deathmatch","Squad Conquest"],"place":",","date":"1942","terrain":"Desert","desc":"Set in Libya, this battlefield portrays a U.S. assault on a North African city occupied by German troops. This is an infantry-only map, where you can expect both close-quarter fights and ranged combat depending on where you are on the battlefield."}]};
